<Base title="About" current="about" reading description="Christian Weilbach: from philosophy and liquid democracy to machine learning and systems that improve themselves.">
  <div class="spread section">
    <div class="main about">
      <h1>About</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        I keep asking how things learn, from cells and societies to machines, and I keep getting better tools for the
        question. Philosophy, neural networks, probabilistic programs and simulations each let me reason about a little
        more of it. The structure of the process stays the same across all of them, and that structure is what I study.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the questions come from</h2>
      <p>
        I came to machine learning from philosophy. I have always cared about constructivism, the view that a learner builds its picture of the world through its
        own activity. Hegel taught me to see history as a learning process
        that grows by reflecting on itself. Bogdanov's tektology, Stafford Beer's cybernetics and Heinz von Foerster's
        work describe organisations and observers as learners of this kind. Critical theory, from Horkheimer and Adorno
        to Žižek, gave me a way to test these ideas, because it asks whose ends a rational system serves.
      </p>
      <p>
        I do not think the tension between instrumental, bounded rationality and reflection on purpose can be resolved
        once. It returns wherever intelligent organisations emerge, at human level or beyond. Building such systems
        includes building the places where that tension can be resolved into a new unified level of abstraction.
      </p>
      <p>
        I do not privilege cooperation or competition. Which of the two serves the people involved depends on the
        situation, and it is often unclear in advance. Better programming models for simulation help people examine and explore
        such situations before they commit.
      </p>

      <h2>From philosophy to programming</h2>
      <p>
        After a year of mathematics and computer science, I studied philosophy and cultural anthropology at Heidelberg
        University from 2006 to 2011.
        What turned me into a programmer was <a href="http://reluk.ca/project/Votorola/home-2013.html">Votorola</a>,
        liquid democracy software designed by my friend Michael Allan with Occupy and other liquid democracy groups
        between 2007 and 2014. It made me want to work on a form of automation that is amenable to collective
        governance and transparent self-optimisation, and I still work on that.
      </p>
      <p>
        Votorola needed a data model that could be distributed across many participants. That led me to Clojure
        and persistent data structures, to <a href="https://github.com/replikativ">replikativ</a> in 2013, to a
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.05545">paper on conflict resolution in open replication</a> with Konrad Kühne
        and Annette Bieniusa, and eventually to <a href="https://datahike.io">Datahike</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Learning machines</h2>
      <p>
        In the meantime I also returned to computer science at Heidelberg. My Bachelor's thesis was on online learning for Boltzmann
        machines on spiking neuron models, which I extended as a research assistant in the Human Brain Project. My
        Master's thesis was on dictionary learning with Bayesian GANs for few-shot classification.
        I co-organised the Clojure meetup in Mannheim-Heidelberg and the Machine Learning Meetup Rhein-Neckar, and in 2018
        founded LambdaForge to develop Datahike and support it in production.
      </p>
      <p>
        For my PhD I moved to Vancouver and the <a href={thesis.group.url}>PLAI group</a> of
        <a href={thesis.advisor.url}>Frank Wood</a> at the University of British Columbia, where I worked on projecting the
        structure of a problem into the neural network architectures that solve it.
      </p>

      <h2>History, language and how communities remember</h2>
      <p>
        With my partner and historian <a href="https://gisellegonzalezgarcia.github.io/portfolio/">Dr. Giselle González García</a> I have worked on what language models can do for
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10808">research in history</a>, and built two open databases of migration on
        this stack, the <a href="https://gisellegonzalezgarcia.github.io/ciid/">Cuba–Ireland Immigration Database</a> and
        <a href="https://gisellegonzalezgarcia.github.io/redes_islenas/">Redes Isleñas</a>, on Canarian emigration to the
        Americas.
      </p>

      <h2>Now</h2>
      <p>
        I am the CEO of <a href="https://simm.is">Simmis</a>, where we build software for organisations of people and AI
        agents. The research on this site continues there and in the open-source projects underneath it.
      </p>
